+  What was built
+
+  A complete multi-tenant analytics dashboard API across all 5 feature areas, over a shared core (db, errors, auth, types, app):
+
+  - Users — register, login (JWT {sub, tid, iat, exp}), /me, admin-only role change. Passwords hashed with Node's built-in scrypt (no extra dependency).
+  - Ingest — single + atomic batch event ingestion.
+  - Query — count, timeseries, breakdown, funnel (per-session greedy step-matching with a rowid tiebreaker for monotonic counts).
+  - Dashboards — full dashboard + widget CRUD.
+  - Export — CSV (RFC-4180 escaping) / JSON event export, dashboard export.
+
+  Tenant isolation is enforced on every query; cross-tenant :id access returns 404. Routers stay auth-agnostic (read req.user only) so both the unit tests' mock auth and the real JWT middleware compose cleanly.
+
+  Beyond the tests (ultracode adversarial review)
+
+  After the 50 tests passed on the first run, I ran a 24-agent review workflow (5 dimensions, each finding independently verified). It confirmed 8 real latent defects the test suite didn't cover — all now fixed and
+  individually regression-tested:
+
+  1. Time-range filters mis-counted ms-precision events at second-aligned boundaries (lexicographic string compare) → now compares parsed instants.
+  2. Empty-string start/end silently zeroed results → treated as unbounded.
+  3. Malformed/oversized JSON bodies returned 500 → error handler now honors framework-attached 4xx statuses.
+  4. Batch ingest of a null item returned 500 → now a clean 400, still atomic.
+  5. Breakdown with a malformed property_key returned 500 and leaked the SQL path → now 400.
+  6. Breakdown collapsed JSON booleans into 1/0 → disambiguated via json_type.
+  7. Widgets lacked an Update endpoint (spec: "Full CRUD for widgets") → added PATCH /dashboards/:id/widgets/:widgetId.
+
+  These were hardening improvements layered on top of an already-passing implementation; none regressed the original contract.
